1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,400
Block two.

2
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:04,650
Can we learn from sequences without labels?

3
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:09,925
On the page, this block is the section "Can we learn from sequences without labels?".

4
00:00:09,925 --> 00:00:17,025
This block answers the session's second question, and it contains the architecture that replaced everything in Block one.

5
00:00:17,025 --> 00:00:30,050
We will start from the economics of labels, meet language models through one you have used yourself, build self-attention from scratch, assemble the Transformer, and then look inside a protein language model to see what it knows.

6
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:36,000
Here is the economic fact that drives the whole of modern machine learning in biology.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,375
Labels are expensive.

8
00:00:38,375 --> 00:00:44,750
Every experimentally supported signal peptide in our dataset is somebody doing an experiment.

9
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:48,575
There are one thousand nine hundred and thirty-six of them.

10
00:00:48,575 --> 00:00:55,025
That is a career's worth of work across a field, and it is not going to increase by a factor of a thousand.

11
00:00:55,025 --> 00:00:57,900
Sequences are abundant and free.

12
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:02,050
UniProt, from Session three, holds hundreds of millions.

13
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:09,150
They arrive by the million from sequencing projects that were done for other reasons, and nobody has to label anything.

14
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:17,050
So the question that defines this block is: can a model learn useful biology from the sequences alone, with no labels at all?

15
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:25,250
And the answer, which would have sounded implausible fifteen years ago, is yes, and the mechanism is almost embarrassingly simple.

16
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:29,175
You ask the model to predict missing or future residues.

17
00:01:29,175 --> 00:01:34,725
The sequence provides its own training signal, because the answer is the part you hid.

18
00:01:34,725 --> 00:01:37,725
That is called self-supervised learning.

19
00:01:38,325 --> 00:01:44,075
Let me explain language models through one you have used, because you already have the intuition.

20
00:01:44,075 --> 00:01:47,300
Your phone's keyboard suggests the next word.

21
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:50,175
ChatGPT writes paragraphs.

22
00:01:50,175 --> 00:01:57,250
Both are doing the same thing: given everything so far, produce a probability for every possible next token.

23
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:03,125
To write, such a model predicts, picks a likely token, appends it to what it has, and repeats.

24
00:02:03,125 --> 00:02:06,275
That loop is the whole of text generation.

25
00:02:06,275 --> 00:02:10,000
The name GPT spells out exactly what it is.

26
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,750
Generative: it produces text.

27
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:25,725
Pre-trained: it first learns from enormous amounts of unlabelled text; GPT-3, in twenty twenty, had a hundred and seventy-five billion weights and was trained on something like three hundred billion tokens.

28
00:02:25,725 --> 00:02:28,875
Transformer: the architecture of this block.

29
00:02:28,875 --> 00:02:36,200
Now here is the part worth pausing on, because it explains why a trivial-sounding task produces something impressive.

30
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:42,275
To continue the sentence "the capital of Sweden is", the model must know a fact about the world.

31
00:02:42,275 --> 00:02:48,675
To continue a sentence that began three paragraphs earlier, it must track what the paragraph is about.

32
00:02:48,675 --> 00:02:57,125
Predicting the next token well is not a shallow skill: doing it excellently requires grammar, facts, and something that behaves like reasoning.

33
00:02:57,125 --> 00:03:00,550
One clarification, since people conflate them.

34
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:15,700
ChatGPT, from November twenty twenty-two, is a GPT model that was then trained further on example conversations and on human ratings of its answers, so that it responds like an assistant instead of simply continuing your text.

35
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:19,950
The base model predicts; the extra training makes it answer.

36
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:23,450
And for proteins, everything carries straight over.

37
00:03:23,450 --> 00:03:26,125
The tokens are amino acids.

38
00:03:26,125 --> 00:03:29,950
The vocabulary is twenty instead of fifty thousand.

39
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:32,400
The data is UniProt.

40
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:41,050
And the claim is the same: to predict a hidden residue in the middle of a signal peptide, the model must learn that this region is hydrophobic.

41
00:03:41,050 --> 00:03:44,650
The biology is forced on it by the prediction task.

42
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:52,075
There are two ways to set up that self-supervised task, and the difference decides what the model can do afterwards.

43
00:03:52,075 --> 00:03:55,525
Autoregressive, which is the GPT style.

44
00:03:55,525 --> 00:03:58,825
Predict the next token from the ones before it.

45
00:03:58,825 --> 00:04:01,700
The model only ever sees the past.

46
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:09,975
That is a restriction, and it is also exactly what makes generation possible: you can run it forward, one token at a time, for ever.

47
00:04:09,975 --> 00:04:15,300
Masked, which is the BERT style, and which ESM-2 uses for proteins.

48
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:21,725
Hide some of the tokens at random, and ask the model to predict them from everything around them, both sides.

49
00:04:21,725 --> 00:04:30,425
The model sees the full context, which makes it better at understanding a sequence it is given, and means it cannot straightforwardly generate.

50
00:04:30,425 --> 00:04:37,475
You have met this distinction already today, one block ago: the forward LSTM against the bidirectional one.

51
00:04:37,475 --> 00:04:40,075
Same trade, different century.

52
00:04:40,075 --> 00:04:46,500
And the thing that makes either worth doing: to predict well, a model has to learn what tends to go with what.

53
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:56,825
Those regularities end up stored in its internal vectors, which we call embeddings, and it turns out those vectors are useful for tasks the model was never trained on.

54
00:04:56,825 --> 00:04:59,875
That is the entire payoff of this block.

55
00:05:00,475 --> 00:05:09,075
Before the real thing, the notebook builds a tiny protein language model, and its result is worth seeing precisely because it is unimpressive.

56
00:05:09,075 --> 00:05:15,450
We measure in bits per residue: how surprised the model is, on average, by the next amino acid.

57
00:05:15,450 --> 00:05:17,375
Lower is better.

58
00:05:17,375 --> 00:05:25,000
Guessing uniformly among twenty amino acids costs log to base two of twenty, which is four point three two two bits.

59
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:34,725
Knowing only the overall amino-acid composition, that is, knowing that leucine is common and tryptophan is rare, gets you to four point one six four bits.

60
00:05:34,725 --> 00:05:41,250
And an LSTM that actually reads the previous residues: four point zero seven one bits.

61
00:05:41,250 --> 00:05:46,825
So reading the sequence buys about a tenth of a bit over simply knowing which letters are common.

62
00:05:46,825 --> 00:05:53,650
That is a small gain, and I show it to make a point: the idea of a language model is not magic in itself.

63
00:05:53,650 --> 00:05:57,775
A small model on a small amount of data learns very little.

64
00:05:57,775 --> 00:06:03,025
What turns this into something transformative is scale, and a better architecture.

65
00:06:03,025 --> 00:06:07,200
The better architecture is attention, and it is the next slide.

66
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:12,275
Self-attention, which is the central mechanism of everything modern.

67
00:06:12,275 --> 00:06:15,225
It is four steps, and each one is simple.

68
00:06:15,225 --> 00:06:18,425
We are computing a new vector for residue i.

69
00:06:18,425 --> 00:06:24,275
Step one: compare residue i with every residue j in the sequence, including itself.

70
00:06:24,275 --> 00:06:28,625
Step two: that comparison gives each j a relevance score for i.

71
00:06:28,625 --> 00:06:34,175
Step three: push those scores through a softmax, so they are all positive and sum to one.

72
00:06:34,175 --> 00:06:36,200
Now they are weights.

73
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:42,950
Step four: update residue i to be a weighted average of the values of all the residues, using those weights.

74
00:06:42,950 --> 00:06:44,625
That is it.

75
00:06:44,625 --> 00:06:51,975
Each position looks at every other position, decides how relevant each one is, and mixes them together accordingly.

76
00:06:51,975 --> 00:06:55,325
Think about what this gives you compared with Block one.

77
00:06:55,325 --> 00:07:05,325
A recurrent network has to carry information from position ten to position two hundred by passing it along a hundred and ninety times, surviving each step.

78
00:07:05,325 --> 00:07:13,225
Attention lets position two hundred look directly at position ten, in one operation, with nothing in between to degrade it.

79
00:07:13,225 --> 00:07:23,375
The weights drawn on this slide are illustrative, but the notebook computes real ones for a toy sentence and checks that each row of the attention matrix sums to one point zero.

80
00:07:23,975 --> 00:07:28,125
Now, how is the comparison in step one actually computed?

81
00:07:28,125 --> 00:07:34,350
Through three learned transformations, and the names are worth getting straight because they are used everywhere.

82
00:07:34,350 --> 00:07:40,250
Each position's vector is turned into three different vectors by three learned linear maps.

83
00:07:40,250 --> 00:07:42,875
The query: what am I looking for?

84
00:07:42,875 --> 00:07:45,325
The key: what do I have to offer?

85
00:07:45,325 --> 00:07:49,275
The value: what do I actually contribute if I am selected?

86
00:07:49,275 --> 00:07:57,800
The score of position j for position i is the match between i's query and j's key, computed as a dot product, which is Session one.

87
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:01,625
The analogy that makes it stick is a database lookup.

88
00:08:01,625 --> 00:08:03,650
You come with a query.

89
00:08:03,650 --> 00:08:05,975
Every entry has a key.

90
00:08:05,975 --> 00:08:16,850
You compare your query with all the keys, and instead of taking the single best match, as a database would, you take a weighted blend of all the values in proportion to how well their keys matched.

91
00:08:16,850 --> 00:08:25,450
Written for all positions at once, it is one line: softmax of Q K transpose divided by the square root of d k, all times V.

92
00:08:25,450 --> 00:08:34,000
The division by the square root of the key dimension is housekeeping: without it the dot products grow with dimension and the softmax saturates.

93
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:45,000
In the notebook, for a six-token toy sentence, Q, K and V are each one by six by eight, and the attention matrix is six by six: every token against every token.

94
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:50,650
Three properties of attention, and then one warning that I care about a great deal.

95
00:08:50,650 --> 00:08:54,525
First: each row of the attention matrix sums to one.

96
00:08:54,525 --> 00:09:02,675
Every position distributes a fixed budget of attention across the sequence; it can concentrate it on one position or spread it thinly.

97
00:09:02,675 --> 00:09:05,650
Second: any distance, in one step.

98
00:09:05,650 --> 00:09:14,825
Position one and position five hundred are exactly as far apart as neighbours, which is the thing recurrent networks and convolutions cannot offer.

99
00:09:14,825 --> 00:09:18,275
Third: all positions are computed in parallel.

100
00:09:18,275 --> 00:09:23,925
A recurrent network must process position one before position two, by construction.

101
00:09:23,925 --> 00:09:31,550
Attention does not, which is precisely why these models could be scaled up on GPUs, and therefore why they took over.

102
00:09:31,550 --> 00:09:35,500
The architecture won partly because it suited the hardware.

103
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:37,325
Now the warning.

104
00:09:37,325 --> 00:09:47,300
You will see papers, and talks, showing attention maps from protein language models with contacts marked on them, and the implication that the model has learned the structure.

105
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:53,900
A high attention weight means that position j contributed strongly when position i was updated.

106
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:56,000
That is all it means.

107
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:02,350
It is not proof of a three-dimensional contact, and it is not an explanation of the model's behaviour.

108
00:10:02,350 --> 00:10:07,025
Attention maps are suggestive and they are not evidence by themselves.

109
00:10:07,025 --> 00:10:14,550
The second discussion problem asks you to measure how much structural signal they actually carry, which is the right way to settle it.

110
00:10:15,150 --> 00:10:19,225
And here is the glaring hole in what I have described so far.

111
00:10:19,225 --> 00:10:23,125
Attention computes a weighted average over positions.

112
00:10:23,125 --> 00:10:26,100
An average does not care about order.

113
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:30,375
So take the sequence A, C, D, E and the sequence E, D, C, A.

114
00:10:30,375 --> 00:10:33,275
Run pure self-attention on both.

115
00:10:33,275 --> 00:10:37,950
You get the same set of output vectors, just in a different arrangement.

116
00:10:37,950 --> 00:10:40,800
The mechanism is blind to order.

117
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:43,700
For a protein that is catastrophic.

118
00:10:43,700 --> 00:10:45,925
Order is the sequence.

119
00:10:45,925 --> 00:10:55,700
A signal peptide at the N-terminus and the same residues at the C-terminus are completely different objects, which is the problem this whole session started from.

120
00:10:55,700 --> 00:11:06,975
The fix is to put the position information into the input: each position's vector gets a position encoding added to it, so that the model can tell position three from position three hundred.

121
00:11:06,975 --> 00:11:16,300
There are several schemes, some fixed and some learned, and the details are less important than knowing the problem exists and that something must be done about it.

122
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:22,275
I like this slide because it shows that attention on its own is not a complete architecture.

123
00:11:22,275 --> 00:11:28,250
It is a very good mechanism with a specific blind spot, which has to be patched deliberately.

124
00:11:28,850 --> 00:11:36,725
The Transformer is self-attention plus four additions, and here is the pleasing part: you already know three of them from this course.

125
00:11:36,725 --> 00:11:38,650
Multiple heads.

126
00:11:38,650 --> 00:11:47,375
Instead of one attention computation, run several in parallel with different learned queries, keys and values, and concatenate the results.

127
00:11:47,375 --> 00:11:51,575
Different heads can attend to different kinds of relationship at once.

128
00:11:51,575 --> 00:11:55,675
A feed-forward network applied to each position separately.

129
00:11:55,675 --> 00:12:00,900
That is Session seven's multilayer perceptron, sitting inside every block.

130
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:10,150
The division of labour is worth remembering: attention moves information between positions, and the feed-forward network processes it within a position.

131
00:12:10,150 --> 00:12:13,825
Residual connections and layer normalisation.

132
00:12:13,825 --> 00:12:25,225
The residual connection is Session ten's ResNet, and it is here for exactly the same reason: without a path for the gradient that skips the block, you cannot train dozens of blocks stacked up.

133
00:12:25,225 --> 00:12:30,750
Layer normalisation re-centres the activations so that the scale does not drift.

134
00:12:30,750 --> 00:12:34,125
And position encoding, from the previous slide.

135
00:12:34,125 --> 00:12:37,300
So the Transformer is not one invention.

136
00:12:37,300 --> 00:12:45,250
It is attention, plus Session seven's MLP, plus Session ten's residual connections, plus a fix for the ordering problem.

137
00:12:45,250 --> 00:12:48,950
Which is why it is worth having done this course in order.

138
00:12:48,950 --> 00:12:59,500
One technical note: many newer models, ESM-2 among them, apply the layer normalisation before each sub-layer rather than after, which trains more stably at depth.

139
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:04,925
Let me give you the actual sizes, because scale is the whole story of this field.

140
00:13:04,925 --> 00:13:17,625
The ESM-2 model used in the notebook: twelve blocks stacked on top of each other, twenty attention heads, four hundred and eighty numbers representing each residue, and thirty-three and a half million weights.

141
00:13:17,625 --> 00:13:25,725
Each block is identical in form: attention, then the per-position feed-forward network, with residual connections around both.

142
00:13:25,725 --> 00:13:35,400
One vector per residue goes in, one vector per residue comes out, and the vectors get progressively more informed about their context as they pass up the stack.

143
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,600
That is the small one.

144
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:42,500
The largest ESM-2 has forty-eight blocks and fifteen billion weights.

145
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,825
And keep in mind what is being computed here.

146
00:13:45,825 --> 00:13:49,700
The output is not a prediction of anything biological.

147
00:13:49,700 --> 00:13:52,375
It is a vector per residue.

148
00:13:52,375 --> 00:13:57,950
The usefulness of those vectors to other tasks is the subject of the rest of the session.

149
00:13:58,550 --> 00:14:04,125
A clarifying slide, because these two names are used as though they were two different fields.

150
00:14:04,125 --> 00:14:10,275
The architectural difference between a GPT model and a BERT model is, essentially, one mask.

151
00:14:10,275 --> 00:14:18,550
A GPT-style decoder masks the future: when computing position i, the attention is only allowed to look at positions up to i.

152
00:14:18,550 --> 00:14:26,850
That restriction is what lets it generate, because generation means producing position i when the future genuinely does not exist yet.

153
00:14:26,850 --> 00:14:33,750
A BERT-style encoder lets every position attend to every other, and learns by filling in masked tokens.

154
00:14:33,750 --> 00:14:40,600
It cannot generate in the straightforward way, and it is better suited to understanding a sequence you hand it whole.

155
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:43,375
ESM-2 is of this kind.

156
00:14:43,375 --> 00:14:50,225
And notice, for the third time this session, that this is the forward versus bidirectional trade from Block one.

157
00:14:50,225 --> 00:14:55,325
Forward LSTM and GPT: can generate, cannot see ahead.

158
00:14:55,325 --> 00:15:01,125
Bidirectional LSTM and BERT: can see everything, cannot generate.

159
00:15:01,125 --> 00:15:08,300
For annotating a protein you already have, which is most of what we do in this course, you want the bidirectional kind.

160
00:15:08,900 --> 00:15:17,000
A short history, because the line from language to biology is more direct than people assume, and parts of it run through this field.

161
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:28,600
Attention was first invented as an addition to recurrent translation models: the decoder was allowed to look back at the encoder's states rather than squeezing a whole sentence into one vector.

162
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:40,725
Then the Transformer paper threw away the recurrence and kept only the attention, which is what made processing all positions in parallel possible, which is what made training on enormous corpora practical.

163
00:15:40,725 --> 00:15:43,750
And a historical detail that I enjoy.

164
00:15:43,750 --> 00:15:52,975
Before SignalP existed, the group behind it used a network that erased the central residue of a window and predicted it from the surrounding residues.

165
00:15:52,975 --> 00:16:03,000
Nielsen and colleagues point out in a recent retrospective that this is, presumably, the first machine learning language model in biological sequence analysis.

166
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,950
Masked language modelling, in the early nineteen nineties, in our own field.

167
00:16:07,950 --> 00:16:10,525
Then two results that mattered.

168
00:16:10,525 --> 00:16:20,275
Rao and colleagues found that the attention maps of protein language models contain contact information, although the models were never trained on any structure.

169
00:16:20,275 --> 00:16:29,575
And the largest ESM-2 models turned out to predict three-dimensional structure directly, which is ESMFold, and which is Session twelve.

170
00:16:30,175 --> 00:16:34,325
So: does a protein language model actually know any biology?

171
00:16:34,325 --> 00:16:36,650
Here is a direct test.

172
00:16:36,650 --> 00:16:42,525
Take ESM-2 and ask it to fill in masked positions, and look at what it proposes.

173
00:16:42,525 --> 00:16:49,575
In the hydrophobic h-region of a signal peptide, the model gives leucine a probability of nought point four one.

174
00:16:49,575 --> 00:16:55,700
Leucine is the archetypal h-region residue, and the model is strongly confident about it.

175
00:16:55,700 --> 00:17:01,675
Now do the same in a cytoplasmic protein: no residue gets above nought point one one.

176
00:17:01,675 --> 00:17:09,925
The model is appropriately uncertain, because in a generic cytoplasmic context there is far less constraint on which residue should be there.

177
00:17:09,925 --> 00:17:17,825
So the model has learned that a particular kind of region is strongly constrained to be hydrophobic, and that other contexts are not.

178
00:17:17,825 --> 00:17:21,100
And nobody told it about signal peptides.

179
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:26,975
There is no label anywhere in its training; it saw sequences and was asked to fill in blanks.

180
00:17:26,975 --> 00:17:37,300
Which is the same discovery as Session ten's filter finding the hydrophobic core, arrived at from a completely different direction, and with no supervision at all this time.

181
00:17:37,900 --> 00:17:43,900
Here is what I think is the single most important idea in this block, so I will say it slowly.

182
00:17:43,900 --> 00:17:50,375
With one-hot encoding, every leucine in every protein is represented by the identical vector.

183
00:17:50,375 --> 00:17:52,725
Leucine is leucine.

184
00:17:52,725 --> 00:17:58,675
The representation carries no information about where that leucine is or what surrounds it.

185
00:17:58,675 --> 00:18:04,175
With a protein language model, each residue's vector is computed from its surroundings.

186
00:18:04,175 --> 00:18:13,700
A leucine in the middle of a signal peptide and a leucine in a cytoplasmic loop get different vectors, because the attention stack mixed in different neighbours.

187
00:18:13,700 --> 00:18:18,825
That is called a contextual embedding, and it is the thing that changed this field.

188
00:18:18,825 --> 00:18:22,925
Think about what it does to the problem we have been working on all session.

189
00:18:22,925 --> 00:18:30,025
Session ten's convolution had to detect the hydrophobic pattern and then, because of pooling, lost where it was.

190
00:18:30,025 --> 00:18:37,650
A contextual embedding has the context baked into the representation of each position before any classifier sees it.

191
00:18:37,650 --> 00:18:45,150
You are no longer asking a classifier to work out the context; you are handing it a representation that already encodes it.

192
00:18:45,150 --> 00:18:56,625
And you have met this idea once before, in a different guise: Foldseek's 3Di alphabet in Session six, which encoded each residue by its structural environment rather than its identity.

193
00:18:56,625 --> 00:18:59,300
Same principle, learned differently.

194
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:04,150
And here is that claim, measured, which is the kind of thing I like.

195
00:19:04,150 --> 00:19:13,450
Take pairs of leucines and measure the cosine similarity of their vectors, which is Session one's dot product normalised, running from minus one to one.

196
00:19:13,450 --> 00:19:19,200
With one-hot encoding, any two leucines have a cosine similarity of exactly one.

197
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:21,425
They are the same vector.

198
00:19:21,425 --> 00:19:23,675
There is nothing to measure.

199
00:19:23,675 --> 00:19:31,525
With ESM-2: two leucines that are both inside signal peptides have a similarity of nought point seven four five.

200
00:19:31,525 --> 00:19:37,375
A signal-peptide leucine against a cytoplasmic leucine: nought point six three three.

201
00:19:37,375 --> 00:19:40,450
So the representation knows the difference.

202
00:19:40,450 --> 00:19:46,725
Leucines in similar contexts have more similar vectors than leucines in different contexts.

203
00:19:46,725 --> 00:19:52,025
And a third comparison that is more interesting still, because it is the hard case.

204
00:19:52,025 --> 00:19:57,600
Two leucines in transmembrane helices: nought point seven four two with each other.

205
00:19:57,600 --> 00:20:03,275
A transmembrane leucine against a signal-peptide leucine: nought point six eight two.

206
00:20:03,275 --> 00:20:05,550
Look at that last number.

207
00:20:05,550 --> 00:20:15,525
Transmembrane and signal-peptide leucines are more similar to each other, nought point six eight, than signal-peptide and cytoplasmic leucines are, nought point six three.

208
00:20:15,525 --> 00:20:22,075
Which is biologically right: both are hydrophobic stretches, and they are genuinely similar environments.

209
00:20:22,075 --> 00:20:28,225
But they are still distinguished, which is exactly the distinction our Session ten network could not make.

210
00:20:28,225 --> 00:20:36,225
This is what a classifier on top of a protein language model gets to work with, and it is why Block three's results come out as they do.

211
00:20:36,825 --> 00:20:42,350
The general recipe, which by now you have met three times in this course under different names.

212
00:20:42,350 --> 00:20:47,525
Pretrain one large model, once, on an enormous amount of unlabelled data.

213
00:20:47,525 --> 00:20:58,175
Then reuse it for many different tasks, each with its own small labelled dataset: either by freezing it and training a small classifier on its outputs, or by fine-tuning it.

214
00:20:58,175 --> 00:21:11,800
In Session ten this was transfer learning: a frozen ImageNet ResNet, and DINOv2 trained on a hundred and forty-two million unlabelled images, both beating a network trained from scratch on a few hundred tissue tiles.

215
00:21:11,800 --> 00:21:14,875
This is that recipe, for sequences.

216
00:21:14,875 --> 00:21:17,275
Two things to keep hold of.

217
00:21:17,275 --> 00:21:24,925
It helps most exactly when labelled data are scarce, which is the common case in biology, and which Block three measures.

218
00:21:24,925 --> 00:21:28,275
And it does not remove the need for an honest split.

219
00:21:28,275 --> 00:21:42,600
A pretrained model makes it harder to reason about leakage, not easier, as Session eight's closing question pointed out: proving that a model trained on all of UniProt never saw your test protein is essentially impossible from outside.

220
00:21:42,600 --> 00:21:48,450
It never saw its label, which is the thing that matters, but be careful about what you claim.

